WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.500
Staying current without drowning.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:10.233
The field produces more each week than anyone can read, and the anxiety of falling behind is itself a productivity killer.

3
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:13.333
Top minds do not read everything.

4
00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:17.233
They run a filter that turns the firehose into three questions.

5
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:19.000
About four hours a week.

6
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.800
That actually compounds.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.367
First, follow builders, not announcers.

8
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:26.476
Announcers repost benchmarks.

9
00:00:26.476 --> 00:00:29.033
Builders ship, and explain what they did.

10
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:31.900
A handful of builders beats a hundred accounts.

11
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.900
Quality of feed beats quantity.

12
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.867
Second, wait two weeks on hype.

13
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:39.200
A post says this changes everything.

14
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.800
You wait, and let others spend their time on it.

15
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.600
Either the result gets reproduced, and now it is worth reading.

16
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.367
Or it quietly fades, and you lost nothing.

17
00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:51.333
Third, depth beats recency.

18
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:54.575
Fundamentals sit at the bottom, and age slowly.

19
00:00:54.575 --> 00:00:58.267
Understanding attention deeply is worth more in five years.

20
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:04.900
Above that, the changelogs of your five tools, like vLLM, LangGraph, and your provider's API.

21
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.300
At the top is the leaderboard, which ages in days.

22
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.300
Here is how it looks in practice.

23
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.567
A launch post claims a huge win.

24
00:01:13.867 --> 00:01:15.001
You do not chase it.

25
00:01:15.001 --> 00:01:18.233
You read the release notes of the tools you actually use.

26
00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:22.433
Two weeks later you check whether anyone reproduced it, and then decide.

27
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:24.388
Let's recap.

28
00:01:24.388 --> 00:01:26.400
Follow builders, not announcers.

29
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.567
Read the release notes of your tools.

30
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:30.200
Wait two weeks on hype.

31
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.100
And depth beats recency.

32
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.233
Next up: the compounding habits.
